{
 "id": "l10",
 "label": "10",
 "title": "The question word ba",
 "summary": "Ba, the spoken question mark: where it goes, ba'y, the particles na, pa, din, daw and nga, and bang",
 "minutes": 35,
 "goals": [
  "Understand what ba does in a question",
  "Place ba correctly in normal and inverted sentences",
  "Place ba after a question word",
  "Know the particles na, pa, din/rin, daw/raw and ngâ, and how they push ba back",
  "Use bang after gustó, áyaw, íbig and other pseudo verbs"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "What ba does",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Some words in Tagalog have no exact English meaning. Instead they add a feeling to what is said, such as curiosity, frustration or irritation. They spice up everyday speech, and a learner does well to understand them."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**Ba** is one of them. It has no specific meaning and cannot be translated into English. It works as a **spoken question mark**: it shows clearly that the speaker is asking a question."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "A question does not have to contain ba. But without it, a question may be mistaken for a statement, especially if it is pronounced wrongly."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Silá ay aalís na.",
       "en": "They will leave now.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Silá",
         "they",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "(links)",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "aalís",
         "leaving",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "na",
         "now",
         "part"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Silá ba ay aalís na?",
       "en": "Are they leaving now?",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Silá",
         "they",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "ba",
         "question word",
         "part"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "(links)",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "aalís",
         "leaving",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "na",
         "now",
         "part"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ],
     "title": "Statement and question"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Use your voice",
     "t": "Remember to **raise the tone of your voice** at the end of a question, to tell it apart from a statement."
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "Where is the focus?"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "The same question may be written **Aalís na ba silá?** This stresses that the action (aalís, leaving) is being confirmed or awaited by the speaker. In **Silá ba ay aalís na?** the focus is on the subject, silá, rather than on the action of leaving."
    },
    {
     "type": "vocab",
     "group": "ba",
     "title": "Ba"
    }
   ]
  },
